+// XE-CURSHADOW: on A7A8 the hardware cursor showed 16-pixel dashes. hwSetCursorImage writes the image with the
+// CPU through controller+0xc98, a CPU mapping (the aperture) of the GGTT range starting at controller+0xdc0, into
+// one of the two cursor buffers at fb+0x1f4[0/1] (GGTT 0x3c00000/0x3c40000). That range is backed by the
+// IOBufferMemoryDescriptor at controller+0xcd0. Tests with XeShim (xe-curpat2..5) showed that what is written
+// through the aperture does not reliably reach the pages the display engine reads (whole runs arrive late or not
+// at all), while the same image stored with direct CPU writes displays cleanly. Flushing the CPU cache did not help.
+// So the whole cursor range is backed by our own write-combined buffer: its GGTT PTEs are remapped to it, and
+// controller+0xc98 is pointed at its direct kernel mapping, so hwSetCursorImage stores the image straight into the
+// pages the display reads. Checked on every image update, so a reallocation by hwSetupCursorMemory is picked up.
+// -ngreennocurshadow turns it off.
+static uint64_t xeGgttPhys(uint32_t gfxAddr) {
+	uint64_t pte = NGreen::callback->readGgttPte(gfxAddr >> 12);
+	return (pte & 1) ? (pte & 0x7FFFFFF000ULL) : 0;
+}
